  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery this morning depicts a trough of low \npressure that extends from Manitoba over to Alberta and moving east. \nCloser to the surface, an associated cold front extends from \nNebraska up through South Dakota before it becomes occluded \nacross North Dakota. Adjacent to this feature, a warm front \nspans central MN into western WI. The cold front is expected to \nsweep through the course of today. Currently, surface obs are \nreporting warm and muggy conditions across much of MN/WI with \ntemps in the lower 70s and dew points sharing near the same \nvalues. Patchy fog has developed across portions of western and \ncentral MN this morning but will mix out shortly after sunrise. \nLight southerly winds shift NW'ly as the front proceeds through \nthe area around midday. Unfortunately the forcing for this \nsystem looks to be aloft versus at the surface. Additionally, \nthe track of the low is too displaced to our north. Therefore, \nprecip chances for today will be practically null. As for \ntemperatures, those in western MN, can expect highs in the lower\n80s while western WI looks to linger in the upper 80s to lower \n90s with heat index values in the 90s. \n\nNow lets talk about dew points. Today's 00z sounding at MPX recorded \na dew point of 73 degrees. Although this comes nowhere near our \nrecord value (81.7 degrees), we are still well above climatology for \nthis time of year (i.e. a mean of 58 degrees for late August). So \naside from this surface front not providing much precip today, dew \npoints are expected to fall back closer to climatology levels for \nmost of the week. Despite temperatures remaining above average,\nheat index values will only range 1-2 degrees warmer than our \nsurface temperatures.\n\nTransitioning to precipitation, our next \"best\" shot at \nbeneficial rainfall looks to be Tuesday night. Latest QPF is \nnothing to get too excited about however trends do look to range\nbetween a tenth and two tenths of an inch across central MN to \nwhereas a few tenths of an inch in for portions of southern MN. \nAfter that, zonal flow will keep us in a holding pattern for \nnightly precip chances. Most areas can expected to stay dry from\nthese periods as coverage would be isolated to scattered at \nbest. Perhaps, stronger forcing could influence greater \nrainfall. Simply put, we need the rain. Our month- to-date \nrainfall deficit as of Aug 31st is -3.76\". So far this year \n(since Jan 1st), we are in a -6.83\" deficit. Meaning, half of \nour drought is just from this month alone. Unless any new precip\nfalls prior to midnight tonight, we'll observe our 4th driest \nAugust on record of 0.46\".",
